2026-09-20 syncfs prerequisite design result at landed Reverie content from https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/608: do not recreate the abandoned userspace SCM_RIGHTS queue ledger from commit 611cb6b0. A ledger limited to sendmsg/recvmsg/recvmmsg cannot remain synchronized because plain read/readv/recvfrom can consume rights, datagrams from external or multiple senders can be observationally indistinguishable after MSG_CTRUNC, stream rights attach to byte boundaries and partial sends, peeks do not consume, and queued socket rights form cycles requiring Linux-like garbage collection. Closing those holes requires broad socket virtualization and new EOPNOTSUPP cases, not a bounded syncfs prerequisite. A kernel-carried marker on the existing file right preserves ancillary count/order/capacity and kernel queue lifetime, but the smallest candidate—sealed memfd plus a versioned HMAC xattr bound to an opaque shmem handle, mount id, birth time, size/content, and exact seals—cannot be claimed at Reverie’s Linux 5.8 floor: tmpfs birth time arrived around 5.18 and unprivileged tmpfs user xattrs around 6.6. Names, seals, content, dev/inode, mount id, OFD flags/locks, or an immortal keeper are individually forgeable, observable, or unbounded. Runtime capability probing with a fail-closed policy is mandatory for that carrier; never fall back to a weak name/inode heuristic. This also exposed a current independent defect: synthetic regular proc snapshots are created O_RDWR without MFD_ALLOW_SEALING even though guest open accepts only O_RDONLY, so host-backed write/pwrite/ftruncate/fallocate can mutate them. Historical https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/534 was correctly closed rather than landing around this identity loss. Follow-up investigations are now testing two narrower routes before declaring a blocker: a Linux-5.8 private-dentry self-authenticating carrier, and whether procfs versus shmem syncfs outcomes are observationally equivalent enough to avoid full identity restoration for this syscall while retaining exact ordinary-fd errors.
